--- !u!114 &60371116
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 60371114}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 58fb7818407ae07408994aa0934587f9, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: '::'
  checklist: {fileID: 1991181456}
  explanations:
  - afterGroupIndex: 1
    delay: 2
    title: "The PC won\u2019t turn on"
    why: "There are no lights, no fan and no sound. Either the power supply isn\u2019t
      giving power, or the \u201Cturn on\u201D signal from the power button isn\u2019t
      reaching the motherboard."
    howToFix: Find out which one it is. Start by testing the PSU on its own. Turn
      off the PSU switch, open the case, and unplug the 24-pin cable from the motherboard.
  - afterGroupIndex: 4
    delay: 2
    title: Testing the PSU by itself
    why: "The 24-pin cable is the PSU\u2019s main connection to the motherboard.
      Unplugging it lets you test the PSU alone, without the motherboard or power
      button involved."
    howToFix: "Bend a paperclip into a U shape. Insert one end into the green wire\u2019s
      hole and the other end into any black wire\u2019s hole. This tricks the PSU
      into thinking the PC was switched on."
  - afterGroupIndex: 7
    delay: 2
    title: The PSU is working
    why: "The green wire is the PSU\u2019s \u201Cturn on\u201D signal and black is
      ground. Connecting them with the paperclip starts the PSU, and since its fan
      spun, the power supply is fine. The problem must be between the power button
      and the motherboard."
    howToFix: 
  - afterGroupIndex: 9
    delay: 2
    title: Check the front panel pins
    why: "The power button on the case is connected to the motherboard by a small
      cable on the front panel header. If it\u2019s on the wrong pins, pressing the
      button does nothing."
    howToFix: Open the motherboard manual and find the pins labeled PWR_SW (power
      switch). Move the power button cable onto those exact pins.
  - afterGroupIndex: 10
    delay: 2
    title: Power switch connected
    why: "The power button cable was on the wrong pins, so the \u201Cturn on\u201D
      signal never reached the motherboard. Now it\u2019s on PWR_SW, so the button
      can start the PC."
    howToFix: Turn the PSU switch back on and press the power button to start the
      PC.
  - afterGroupIndex: 12
    delay: 2
    title: Problem solved
    why: The PSU was never broken. The paperclip test proved it worked, so you avoided
      replacing a good part. The real fault was the power button cable on the wrong
      pins.
    howToFix: Remember to test a part before replacing it. It saves time and money.
