--- !u!114 &1211094084
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1211094082}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 58fb7818407ae07408994aa0934587f9, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: '::'
  checklist: {fileID: 149826874}
  explanations:
  - afterGroupIndex: 1
    delay: 2
    title: The PC overheated
    why: The power supply's fan is clogged with dust, so hot air can't escape. When
      the PSU gets too hot, it shuts the computer down to protect itself.
    howToFix: Turn off the PSU switch first. Then use the compressed air can to blow
      the dust out of the PSU fan, turn the PSU switch back on and press the power
      button.
  - afterGroupIndex: 2
    delay: 2
    title: Why turn off the PSU first?
    why: The PSU keeps sending power to the computer as long as it is switched on
      and plugged in. Cleaning or touching parts while power is still live can cause
      a short circuit, damage the parts, or give you a shock. Turning the PSU switch
      off first cuts that power, so it's safe to work.
    howToFix: 
  - afterGroupIndex: 4
    delay: 2
    title: How to use compressed air can
    why: While grabbing, trigger the click button to use.
    howToFix: 
  - afterGroupIndex: 5
    delay: 2
    title: The fan is clean
    why: With the dust gone, air can flow through the PSU again, which keeps it cool
      while the PC is running.
    howToFix: 
  - afterGroupIndex: 8
    delay: 2
    title: Problem solved
    why: The PSU can cool itself again, so it no longer overheats and the PC stays
      on. Cleaning dust from fans regularly prevents this problem from happening
      again.
    howToFix: 
