  m_text: RAM (Random Access Memory) is your computer's short-term working memory.
    It temporarily holds the data and programs you're actively using so the CPU can
    access them far faster than it could from a storage drive. It's volatile, meaning
    everything in it is erased when power is lost, and more RAM lets you run more
    apps and larger tasks smoothly at once. Think of it as your desk (RAM) versus
    a filing cabinet (storage).
